{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0121\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The multi-unit/shared-space curtilage conflict remains unresolved. Johnson v. United States presented the issue, but certiorari was denied April 20, 2026.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 multi-unit dwelling curtilage decisions and Supreme Court docket 25-774.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0122\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court decision has resolved whether prolonged pole-camera surveillance is a Carpenter-style mosaic search; lower-court disagreement remains.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 pole-camera mosaic cases, Tuggle, Moore-Bush, House, and current certiorari activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0123\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Keith's express reservation of foreign-intelligence surveillance remains historically accurate. FISA supplied a statutory framework, but the Supreme Court has not issued the reserved constitutional ruling.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Keith foreign-intelligence reservation, FISA, and Page v. Comey (D.C. Cir. 2025).\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0124\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Digital plain view and treatment of nonresponsive computer data remain unresolved by the Supreme Court. United States v. Holcomb supplies recent Ninth Circuit authority, not a nationwide rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 digital plain-view decisions, computer-warrant scope, Holcomb, and Supreme Court activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0125\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Recent Holcomb litigation reinforces that lower courts use differing scope, particularity, probable-cause, and plain-view analyses; no Supreme Court decision has unified them.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched digital plain-view approach spectrum, nonresponsive data, Holcomb, and current Supreme Court docket.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0126\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The hash-matching private-search split remains live and has deepened: United States v. Brillhart (11th Cir. 2026) joined the Fifth and Sixth Circuits, while Maher, Lowers, and Wilson take the contrary view.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 hash-matching private-search appellate decisions; found Lowers and Brillhart.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0127\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not resolved whether automated hash matching qualifies as a private search. Brillhart expressly identifies the continuing circuit split.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Supreme Court hash-match/private-search cases and 2026 appellate developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0128\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court decision directly adjudicates a Fourth Amendment search of a tent. Grants Pass concerned the Eighth Amendment, not tent-search privacy.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Supreme Court tent Fourth Amendment cases and 2025-2026 tent-search decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0129\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The permission/legal-occupancy conflict persists. United States v. Parkerson (5th Cir. 2025) denied privacy because the tent occupant was trespassing, while Sandoval remains contrary authority on public-land occupancy.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Ruckman, Sandoval, Parkerson, and 2025-2026 public-land tent cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0130\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Tent-search doctrine remains circuit- and state-built, with no direct Supreme Court Fourth Amendment decision and continuing disagreement over unlawful occupancy.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Supreme Court tent-search precedent, Parkerson, Sandoval, and recent homeless-encampment cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0131\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court decision on cell-site simulators was found; current treatment remains based on lower-court authority, statutes, and policy.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 StingRay/cell-site-simulator Supreme Court and appellate developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0132\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Cell-site-simulator Fourth Amendment rules remain outside controlling Supreme Court precedent.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched current Supreme Court cell-site-simulator cases, certiorari grants, and recent legal surveys.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0133\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court merits case directly addressing a StingRay or cell-site simulator was located.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Supreme Court docket and 2025-2026 cell-site-simulator decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0134\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling decision was found resolving Fourth Amendment rights of bystander phones swept into cell-site-simulator collection; 2026 commentary continues to describe the issue as a legal gray area.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched cell-site-simulator bystander collection, dragnet phones, and recent Fourth Amendment decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0135\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court decision on investigative genetic genealogy was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 IGG Supreme Court, federal appellate, and certiorari developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0136\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court or federal appellate holding deciding whether IGG is a Fourth Amendment search was located. The Kohberger ruling was a state trial-court decision.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched IGG Fourth Amendment appellate decisions and 2025-2026 Kohberger litigation.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0137\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie v. United States (2026) addressed historical Google Location History, not real-time CSLI or tower dumps; those Carpenter reservations remain unresolved.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched post-Chatrie real-time CSLI and tower-dump authority.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0138\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie held that acquisition of Location History is a search but expressly remanded whether each warrant stage satisfied probable cause and particularity. The stated merits question therefore remains open.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reviewed Chatrie v. United States, 609 U.S. ___ (2026), especially its probable-cause/particularity remand.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0139\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie supplied a search-threshold rule but left geofence-warrant probable cause and particularity for lower courts to decide at each stage, preserving case-by-case development.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reviewed the 2026 Chatrie opinion and searched post-decision geofence-warrant merits authority.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0140\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The composite reservation is no longer wholly current. Chatrie held that acquisition of only two hours of Google Location History is a search, materially answering the short-duration location-data issue, while real-time CSLI and tower dumps remain open.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Chatrie v. United States, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Carpenter shorter-period reservations and reviewed Chatrie's two-hour holding.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0141\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Pole-camera aggregation remains divided, ALPR treatment remains fact- and jurisdiction-dependent, and real-time CSLI remains reserved. Chatrie did not decide those technologies.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 pole-camera, ALPR, and real-time-CSLI developments after Chatrie.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0142\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court pole-camera decision has resolved the Tuggle/Moore-Bush divide.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 pole-camera decisions, House, Tuggle, Moore-Bush, and certiorari activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0143\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"ALPR law remains jurisdiction- and network-specific. United States v. Porter (5th Cir. 2026) upheld a limited ten-camera system, while broader-network challenges such as Schmidt remain fact-sensitive.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 ALPR appellate decisions; reviewed Porter and Schmidt developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0144\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"Chatrie materially narrowed the claim that quantity is the live question across every technology: for Google Location History, even a two-hour acquisition is a search, without a duration grace period. Mosaic questions remain for pole cameras and ALPRs.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Chatrie v. United States, 609 U.S. ___ (2026)\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reviewed Chatrie's rejection of a short-duration threshold and searched post-Chatrie mosaic authority.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0145\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court rule specifically governing cell-site simulators was found; warrant requirements remain grounded in analogy, lower-court rulings, legislation, and policy.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 StingRay warrant decisions and Supreme Court activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0146\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The real-time-location question remains unresolved by the Supreme Court. Chatrie involved retrospective Location History.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched real-time CSLI after Carpenter and Chatrie.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0147\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"IGG still lacks controlling Supreme Court or federal appellate search precedent; recent decisions located were state trial-level rulings.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 investigative genetic genealogy controlling precedent.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0148\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Pole-camera aggregation remains a first-impression or split-sensitive issue across jurisdictions; no controlling Supreme Court resolution was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Tuggle, Moore-Bush, House, and 2025-2026 pole-camera aggregation cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0149\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Moore-Bush remains fractured, and no later Supreme Court ruling has resolved its competing pole-camera rationales.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Moore-Bush rationale split and subsequent Supreme Court or circuit resolution.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0150\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The private-search hash-matching split remains live. Brillhart (11th Cir. 2026) expressly aligned with the Fifth and Sixth Circuits against the Second, Fourth, and Ninth Circuits.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Wilson, Maher, Lowers, Reddick, Miller, and Brillhart.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0151\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court holding adopting pure horizontal pooling of uncommunicated officer knowledge was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched horizontal collective knowledge, fellow-officer aggregation, and 2025-2026 Supreme Court developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0152\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Fourth Circuit authority continues to reject pure horizontal aggregation. Ferebee reiterates Massenburg's limitation to communicated instructions or alerts; no later contrary Fourth Circuit or Supreme Court holding was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched current Fourth Circuit collective-knowledge cases citing Massenburg.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0153\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No First Circuit decision was found superseding Balser's express refusal to decide the outer limit of horizontal aggregation.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched post-Balser First Circuit horizontal-aggregation decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0154\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court resolution of uncommunicated horizontal pooling was located; circuits continue to apply differing formulations.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched horizontal pooling, uncommunicated facts, Hensley, and current Supreme Court activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0155\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court holding was found making race part of the Mendenhall free-to-leave or free-to-terminate seizure inquiry. Recent race-based reasonable-suspicion litigation addresses a different issue.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched race, Mendenhall, free-to-leave seizure, Carter, and 2025-2026 Supreme Court decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0156\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court decision was found deciding Hiibel's reserved Fifth Amendment case in which disclosing a name supplies an incriminating evidentiary link.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 compelled-identity and Hiibel link-in-the-chain cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0157\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The armed-versus-dangerous frisk split remains live. Recent Virginia and Maryland decisions continue to contrast Robinson with Northrup and acknowledge the unresolved federal division.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 lawful gun carry frisk cases citing Robinson, Northrup, and Leo.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0158\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No split was found over the settled core public-arrest probable-cause rule. Gonzalez v. United States (2025) addressed the narrower misdemeanor-in-the-officer's-presence refinement.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 warrantless-arrest standards, circuit splits, and Gonzalez.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0159\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The circuits remain divided over whether Payton's 'reason to believe' standard equals probable cause, and no Supreme Court merits resolution or current grant was found.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Payton quantum cases, certiorari petitions, and Supreme Court docket.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0160\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The constructive-entry circuit split remains unresolved. People v. Shaw (N.Y. 2026) recognized coercive constructive entry while noting that Payton does not squarely decide the question.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 surround-and-call-out constructive-entry cases and People v. Shaw.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0161\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No current controlling circuit split was located concerning the basic Gerstein/McLaughlin 48-hour rule; recent material continues to state the same nationwide baseline.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Gerstein hearing and McLaughlin 48-hour application cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0162\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"United States v. Mendoza (3d Cir. 2026) rejected privacy five hours after checkout on its facts but expressly left open whether a hotel-specific grace period exists, so the disagreement remains.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched hotel checkout grace-period standing and reviewed Mendoza.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0163\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later controlling decision was found resolving the precise Lyle reservation. Byrd supplies a general lawful-possession rule, while Lyle turned on the combination of unauthorized and unlicensed driving.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched unauthorized-but-licensed rental drivers after Byrd and reviewed Lyle.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0164\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The active-pursuit split persists. Minnesota v. Steeprock presented the issue, but the Supreme Court denied certiorari on June 8, 2026.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 inevitable-discovery active-pursuit cases and reviewed Supreme Court docket 25-1207.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0165\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court decision or later Ninth Circuit authority was found displacing Cano's rejection of good-faith reliance merely on unsettled law.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 good-faith exception, unsettled law, Cano, and certiorari developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0166\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court decision was found defining all post-attachment events that constitute critical stages requiring counsel; Rothgery's reservation remains operative.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Rothgery post-attachment critical-stage and lineup authority.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0167\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No nationwide resolution was found for either the active-inducement/passive-listener boundary or all precharge attachment scenarios; lower courts continue applying Henry, Kuhlmann, and Rothgery factually.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched recent jailhouse-informant deliberate-elicitation and precharge-attachment cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0168\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The wrong-house qualified-immunity divide remains live. Certiorari in Jimerson v. Lewis was denied February 24, 2025; Martin v. United States (2025) addressed FTCA defenses, not the Jimerson qualified-immunity question.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reviewed Jimerson docket 24-473 and searched Martin and 2025-2026 wrong-house qualified-immunity cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0169\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Barnes expressly left officer-created danger open. The Fifth Circuit's fact-specific ruling on remand and the Supreme Court's April 27, 2026 denial of renewed review did not create a Supreme Court rule.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched Barnes remand, renewed certiorari, and 2026 officer-created-danger developments.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0170\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not decided how an officer's reckless creation of a pre-seizure danger affects force reasonableness. Barnes requires temporal totality but reserved the distinct causation/normative question.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Reviewed Barnes, its Fifth Circuit remand, and post-Barnes pre-seizure-conduct cases.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0171\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"Officer-created-jeopardy treatment remains jurisdiction-specific. Renewed Supreme Court review of Barnes was denied in 2026, leaving circuit approaches intact.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Barnes officer-created-danger circuit treatment and certiorari status.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0172\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No granted or pending Supreme Court merits case centered on consent-search doctrine was found in the current docket searches.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched the 2025-2026 Supreme Court docket for consent search, Jimeno scope, and related certiorari grants.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0173\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court ruling was found resolving a general persons-in-public community-caretaking doctrine. Case v. Montana (2026) concerned the distinct emergency-aid exception for home entry.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched post-Caniglia public-person caretaking cases and reviewed Case v. Montana.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0174\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No recent Supreme Court decision specifically resolving the destruction-of-evidence or manufactured-exigency frontier was found; Kentucky v. King remains the controlling reference point.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 destruction-of-evidence exigency and manufactured-exigency decisions.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0175\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"The Supreme Court still has not decided constructive entry, and the federal circuits remain divided. People v. Shaw (N.Y. 2026) expressly observed that Payton does not squarely address coercive call-outs.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched constructive-entry Supreme Court authority, circuit cases, and People v. Shaw.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0176\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No Supreme Court decision has resolved whether surrounding a home and coercing an occupant outside is a Payton in-home arrest; recent state authority confirms the unresolved federal question.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Payton surround-and-call-out decisions and Supreme Court activity.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0177\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"The asserted unmapped list overlooks representative authority. Sharrar v. Felsing addresses coercive constructive entry in the Third Circuit; United States v. Curzi supplies First Circuit authority; Duncan v. Storie addresses the Eighth Circuit's doorway/voluntariness approach.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Sharrar v. Felsing, 128 F.3d 810 (3d Cir. 1997); United States v. Curzi, 867 F.2d 36 (1st Cir. 1989); Duncan v. Storie, 869 F.2d 1100 (8th Cir. 1989)\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched constructive entry separately for the First, Third, Fourth, and Eighth Circuits; cross-checked authorities cited in People v. Shaw.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0178\",\"verdict\":\"MOVED\",\"basis\":\"There is now a recent nationwide Supreme Court exigency ruling. Case v. Montana held that emergency-aid home entry is valid when officers have an objectively reasonable basis to believe someone inside needs emergency assistance.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"Case v. Montana, 607 U.S. ___ (2026)\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 Supreme Court exigent-circumstances and hot-pursuit decisions; reviewed Case v. Montana.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0179\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No recent Supreme Court decision directly resolving the scene-securing frontier was found. Case v. Montana concerns emergency-aid entry and does not establish a general rule for securing premises pending a warrant.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 securing-the-scene, secure-premises, Segura, and McArthur authority.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0180\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No later Supreme Court decision was found resolving Flores-Montano's reservation for border property searches so destructive that a different Fourth Amendment result may be required.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched 2025-2026 destructive border-property searches and Flores-Montano citations.\"}\n{\"claim_id\":\"ABS-0181\",\"verdict\":\"HOLDS\",\"basis\":\"No controlling Supreme Court decision was found treating device or vehicle-computer data as an automobile-exception container. Riley protects phone data under another exception, while lower authority and scholarship continue to dispute onboard and portable-device data.\",\"contrary_authority\":\"\",\"discovery_path\":\"Searched automobile exception, cell-phone data, onboard computers, infotainment systems, and digital-container decisions.\"}"}}
